<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osei Tutu I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1660–<a href="/years/1717">1717</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Osei Tutu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osei Tutu I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, uniting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of the forest zone of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> around the turn of the eighteenth century. Leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyoko-clan">Oyoko clan</a>'s state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, he worked with the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> became the symbol of Asante unity. Their victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> at Feyiase in 1701 established Asante independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Osei Kofi Tutu was born in the town of Kokofu Anyinam. His father, Owusu Panyin, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> noble from Nyameani, and his mother, Maanu Kotosii, was the sister of the rulers of Kwaaman, Oti Akenten and Obiri Yeboa. Tradition says she was long barren and named her son after Otutu, a shrine in Akuapem where she had sought a blessing. Under the matrilineal succession of the Akan he was heir to the Kwaaman throne.</p><p>As a young man he was sent to Abankeseso, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a>, which held the heirs of its subordinate kings as hostages while training them in statecraft and war; he served there as a shield-bearer to the Denkyirahene. Oral histories tell that an adulterous affair forced him to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a>, whose army, with its musketmen, was the most advanced of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state. Either there or in Denkyira he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When his uncle Obiri Yeboa was killed in a campaign against Dormaa, the nobles of Kwaman invited Osei Tutu home to take the stool, and he returned with Anokye and 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a> warriors. He created new stools for the treasury, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and the royal household, and reorganised the army into seven divisions independent of clan, built around musketmen. He then defeated Dormaa and through the 1680s absorbed Tafo, Kaase, Offinso and other towns, extending citizenship to all regardless of origin.</p><p>When the Denkyirahene Ntim Gyakari demanded gold, hostages and wives, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyoko-clan">Oyoko</a> chiefs filled his brass basin with stones. Osei Tutu united the Amantoo states under his leadership as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>; according to legend, Anokye brought down from the sky a wooden stool partly covered with gold, which held the soul of the nation. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> as the new capital and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odwira-festival">Odwira festival</a>. After winning over many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> defectors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> defeated Ntim Gyakari at Feyiase and sacked Abankeseso for 15 days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wars continued against Akyem and the remnants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a>. In 1711 Osei Tutu sacked Ahwenekokoo, the capital of Wenchi, gaining the trade routes north to Bonoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begho">Begho</a>, and between 1713 and 1715 he conquered Twifo, Wassa and Aowin. In October 1717, campaigning against Akyem, his army was trapped in the forest; he was shot by snipers while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pra-river">River Pra</a>, and his body was never recovered. He had tripled the size of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, and a shrine in Anyinam commemorates his birth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osei Kofi Tutu I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ashanti Empire, more properly the Asante Empire, was founded around the turn of the eighteenth century, when <b>Osei Tutu I</b> deliberately united the fragmented Akan chiefdoms of the forest zone of modern Ghana in a political and spiritual union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1701 <b>Osei Tutu</b> of Asante defeated and killed King Ntim Gyakari at Feyiase, and Denkyira became tributary to the Ashanti Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/denkyira">Denkyira</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Anyinam, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.7,-1.97&amp;year=1697" aria-label="Anyinam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4496 1913 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Osei Tutu I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4946 2183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4946 2183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.7,-1.97&amp;year=1697">What was here in 1697? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
